 "rating_margin": 9.28, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.9, "baseline_margin": 8.83, "analyst_adjustment": -0.83, "key_factors": ["Open seat with PVI favoring Democrats by 3 points in a strong Democratic environment", "Prediction markets price Powell at 92-94% to win", "Powell outraised Harding and holds a large outside-spending advantage", "Only one poll (SurveyUSA, D+4) leaves limited polling evidence"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point clearly to Democrats: a D+3 district, no incumbent on the ballot, a favorable national environment, Powell's fundraising and outside-spending edge, and the Omaha mayor's endorsement. Markets price her around 93%, consistent with a Likely D rating, though the single D+4 poll suggests the margin may be tighter than the fundamentals imply.", "watch": "A strong Harding debate performance or a late shift on affordability could narrow the race toward Lean D.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Don Bacon (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Don Bacon's retirement leaves this Omaha-area district, which leans Democratic by 3 points, open in what appears to be a favorable year for Democrats. Denise Powell has outraised Brinker Harding and benefits from roughly $3 million in outside support, and she has secured the Omaha mayor's endorsement. Harding enters with solid resources of his own and about $1 million to spend.\n\nRaters across the board call the race a Democratic flip, ranging from Tilt D to Safe D, and prediction markets put Powell's chances above 90%. The lone poll shows Powell up four points, suggesting a competitive but Democratic-leaning contest. Affordability has dominated the campaign conversation, with both candidates pitching fixes and trading charges of extremism ahead of their October debates.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9278, "margin": 8.8}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9035, "margin": 8.0}]}